Hi [~accountid:61124fbaec92ab0070270a0b] ,



Regarding the exclusion of liability proposed by Jobantalent, please see below the external counsel’s comments in green:



# {color:#36b37e}_Jobandtalent proposes to add a clause according to which they will not be liable for any act, behavior or omission of the employees that they will allocate to the rendering of the services (e.g. theft or robbery of Bolt’s property or of its clients or suppliers), nor for any act, behavior or omission that causes damage, physical and/or material, to Bolt (or to its clients or suppliers), provided that such acts, behaviors or omissions result from Jobandtalent’s employees willful misconduct or negligence._{color}
# {color:#36b37e}_According to Portuguese law, the employer is liable (regardless of fault) for the acts of its employees. However, if the damages were caused due to employee’s willful misconduct or negligence (e.g. the case of a theft), the employer is legally entitled to claim to the employee the reimbursement of what might have paid or of the damages that might have been caused to it._{color}
# {color:#36b37e}_For this reason, a contractual provision of the type of the one being proposed by Jobandtalent is not in line with the market standard for outsourcing agreements of this kind considering that,_ {color}{color:#36b37e}+_by law_+{color}{color:#36b37e} {color}{color:#36b37e}*_(i)_* {color}{color:#36b37e}_Jobandtalent (as the employer) is the entity liable for the acts of its employees (clause 4 of the services agreement expressly states that Jobandtalent’s employees are subject to the exclusive direction and disciplinary power of Jobandtalent), and_ {color}{color:#36b37e}*_(ii)_*{color}{color:#36b37e} _Jobandtalent’s position is already protected in case of employee’s willful misconduct or negligence (considering that Jobandtalent is entitled to claim reimbursement from the employee, as referred above)._{color}
# {color:#36b37e}_Furthermore and most importantly, in our view, such a contractual provision_ {color}{color:#36b37e}+_significantly increases the risk (previously identified) of requalification of the relationship between Bolt and Jobandtalent’s employees as an employment contract_+{color}{color:#36b37e}_. In fact, considering that the employees allocated to the services are Jobandtalent’s employees and that the services agreement is entered between Jobandtalent and Bolt, there is no reason, in our view, for Jobandtalent not being liable (in any case) before Bolt for the acts of the employees allocated to such services. Stating the opposite can be used to argue that they are, in fact, Bolt’s employees, including the possibility of Bolt being liable for such acts._{color}
# {color:#36b37e}_In light of the above, we recommend_ {color}{color:#36b37e}+_not to accept_+{color}{color:#36b37e} _the paragraph proposed by Jobandtalent, in order to protect Bolt’s position and, therefore, we are not suggesting an alternative wording for the clause. In this regard, please also note that including a numerus clausus in this type of clause does not eliminate the risk identified above._{color}

Please see attached the template of services agreement with a few rectifications. The external counsels have run a quick sanity check to the document and found a few typos.

In addition and comparing to the last version of the draft that they had reviewed, they have found that it was missing one paragraph in Clause 9 which they believe should be included and, therefore, they added it (previously they had added a paragraph 4 to make clear that the termination of the contract by any of the parties would not give rise to a compensation right – where such termination is not due to a breach of the agreement by the other party and is made according to the procedure stipulated in Clause 9, i.e., with 60-day prior notice).



