Hi [~accountid:5ad5a3d0af21cf2a74841d6d] 

Fine re 3.3

4.3 - I meant these provisions in the template:

5.1.2 To comply at the Premises with the fire safety, electrical safety, sanitary and health regulations, a*nd other such legislation and instructions*, and to ensure compliance with the specified obligations even by the employees *and visitors* of Bolt; 

5.1.3 To comply with the general safety requirements established for the building the Premises are located at;

5.1.5 To ensure that it holds, by the moment of commencement of their own business activities, *all the public permits and registrations required for its business activities, and hold these in force during the entire term of the Agreement*;

5.1.6 To ensure that its activities at the Premises are *not contrary to public policy and meet all the public law requirements*;

We are already under the obligation to obtain permits and registrations and comply with all laws and regulations. And as mentioned before, the removal point is also already covered in 11.2. 

However, it goes without saying that Bolt *should only* be carrying out things in accordance with the law and the required permits and licences. Therefore in principle, we cannot argue against this. In the future though, I would resist it as I think this is already adequately covered in our document and we should not have different clauses in the lease saying the same thing. 

5.2.4

_They will_ *_inform us_* _about any interruption of media delivery, like electricity, that is not caused by them or any other Tenant in the premisses. like electrical work done by the power supplier in the area, where there will be no electricity, which is crucial for us._

Fine for them to inform us (but this is *not* what 5.2.4 says\!) and fine for them to exclude liability for temporary interruptions to the provisions of services that are *beyond their reasonable control* (i.e. power cuts)\[5.2.4 already deals with this] - but a contract should *not exclude liability caused by the acts of other parties* - the circumstances are a matter of fact and cannot properly be determined upfront. 

5.2.4 is mostly concerned with indirect and consequential losses e.g. “_damage due to Bolt's failure to conduct business_“ etc. As I said, this is already covered in 13.4. 

Also, 13.2 deals with the liability point as it states that “Either party shall be liable for any damage caused *by the actions of their legal representatives and any authorised persons used by them for the performance of their contractual obligations, including in particular any employees or mandataries,* in the same way as for any damage caused by their own actions.

This means that the Landlord (and Bolt) is responsible for any damage caused by someone it is in control of (and not other parties). This is fair and this applies to both parties. So I do not think any additional drafting is required on this and we should remove 5.2.4 because this is already covered in the lease. 

