Hi\!

I have reviewed the case and gathered background information. As far as I can understand, this situation is caused by inexperienced user who didn’t know how to use the app.

The sum is low, but this is more of a principle - can we allow our users to claim ignorance and ignore our TnCs.

Shot summary of the possible options (from worst to best):

# {color:#ff5630}Pay it back and block accounts - retaliation and bad press, I’d rather avoid this approach.{color}
# {color:#ff5630}Ignore the decision - this decision is unenforceable, but if we continue to ignore the rulings, we can damage our relationship with TTJA and we can end up being blacklisted - bad for business, bad press.{color}
# {color:#ff991f}Pay it back and continue with our business. As this approach contradicts guidelines given by our management, we should avoid this approach - we don’t need to take blame for the cases if we were in the right.{color}
# {color:#36b37e}Filing a complaint to Harju maakohus - we have a right to have the case processed in the court. The filing fee is 75 EUR and if we are doing this internally - we should allocate at least 2 hrs. We can demand the filing fee from the customer, if the ruling is in our favour. The case can be solved by a compromise. {color}

As this is a principal question, some input from the manager would be highly appreciated. From legal POV this is quite straightforward case. This question is intertwined with PR, customer support and overall branding.

We have 7 days to react, otherwise this decision shall become valid and we lose the right for reviewed proceedings.

