{quote}Can we please take this one case as if the client had paid by mistake?{quote}

Previously you have mentioned that this payment has been done on purpose:

{quote}at that time, the company wanted to make an upfront payment and be invoiced against it{quote}

If I understand your description of the situation correctly, the company made an upfront payment to avoid taxes. We allow companies to make upfront payments, but we are not obliged and do not have a technical possibility to refund them fully or partially. As far as I understand from [~accountid:5f1a8e14e5efc1001bf8ec6d] ‘s reply, we cannot move the remaining money from one company’s balance to another either. Thus, unfortunately, we cannot offer any other solution, only to explain our limitations to the company owner and suggest using this amount for the future rides.

[~accountid:5bdbfc9b359ddf204e8c7e02] Optional solution to include bottle deposit on the description would work.

[~accountid:61027984a539cb006846c64f] Please see answers below:

Where 10 cents (which customer pays us) end up? {color:#ff5630}Ideally they end up in customer’s pocket if the customer returns the used bottle to the specifically designated place. If the bottle is not returned to such place, then 10 cents will not be returned to the customer. For us the only important thing is that we display the deposits as {color}

Should we then report those 10 cents somewhere? {color:#ff5630}No, according to accounting we are not keeping any records over the deposits in our inventory system.{color}

Can we ask suppliers to switch non-deposit SKUs to deposit ones? {color:#ff5630}No legal limitations to do that but I doubt they will agree to do this.{color}

Can we ignore that deposit even if we have bought bottle with deposit? (just to ignore until we have only deposit SKUs in stock) {color:#ff5630}I understand from {color}[this post|https://taxify.slack.com/archives/C024SRQA2G6/p1642062208066900]{color:#ff5630} that there will be a transition period for this kind of bottles until 1 August 2022. In that case there are no limitations to ignoring the deposit for the bottles that already exist in our stock.{color}

Please let me know if you have any additional questions.

