Hello [~accountid:606af51d126db9006ff53abe], [~accountid:608fa4805c7212007169efa8],

While thinking about what I am going to verify as part of this integrity check, I identified two main directions which I am going to detail below.

# Validating the values syntax associated with the {{maxspeed}} key
#* {{maxspeed=number}}
#* {{maxspeed=number [measurement unit: km/h | kmh | kph | mph | knots]}}
#* {{maxspeed=none|walk}}
#* {{maxspeed=countrycode:roadtype}}-> this applies to implicit speed limits

Then we have some extra attributes that can be annexed to {{maxspeed}}

* {{maxspeed:variable=yes|no|[reasons separated by semicolon]}}
* {{maxspeed:lanes=[numbers separated by pipes]}}
* {{maxspeed:advisory=number [measurement unit]}}
* {{maxspeed:conditional}}=
** time and date - we will use a special [OpeningHoursParser|https://github.com/simonpoole/OpeningHoursParser] to cover all the possibilities
** weather conditions - wet | snow
** vehicle constraints - e.g. weight < 3.5t
* {{maxspeed:vehicle_type=number [measurement unit]}}

All of these keys can have {{forward}} or {{backward}} appended to them.

We also need to verify the {{minspeed}} key, which is somewhat similar to the {{maxspeed}} one. It can look like {{minspeed=number [measurement unit]}}and it can have {{lanes}} / {{forward}} / {{backward}} attached to it.

2. Some wrong cases that do not make sense from a logical point of view

I am planning to tackle the following cases:

* Both {{maxspeed}} and {{maxspeed:forward/backward}} found.
* {{maxspeed:forward/backward}} tags on oneway roads.



This is what I envision as part of the maxspeed integrity checks. Is there any other case that you can think of that usually pops up and may be bothering? Or, rather, in the grammar side, have I identified too many cases and some of them are not of interest?

Thank you,

Andrei

